::: {.doc-subject}

Check if an undirected graph is a complete split graph.
:::

::: {.doc-usage-source}
Usage

[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L33464-L33571){target="_blank" rel="noopener"}
:::

::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}
```pythonsignature
Graph.is_complete_split_graph()
```
:::

::: {.doc-text}

![Is complete split graph](../assets/graphs/is-complete-split-graph.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt="Left: the clique X, Y (shaded) is joined to every node of the independent set A, B, C, so is_complete_split_graph() returns True. Right: without the edge X-C, drawn dashed in the accent color, C misses part of the clique; the graph is still split but is_complete_split_graph() returns False." width="100%"}

A *complete split graph* is a split graph with every possible edge between its two
parts: a clique whose nodes are joined to everything, and an independent set whose
nodes are joined to the whole clique and to nothing else. Stars (a one-node clique)
and complete graphs (an empty independent set) are the extremes. Its nodes therefore
take only two roles, a core that reaches everyone and a periphery that reaches only the
core, as in a hub-and-spoke network with fully connected hubs.

Parallel edges count once and self-loops are ignored.
:::

## Returns {.doc-returns}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   `True` if the graph is a complete split graph. The empty graph, a single node and
    any graph with no edges (an empty clique) are.
:::

## Raises {.doc-raises}

::: {.doc-definition-items}
<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>

:   If the graph is directed.
:::

## See Also {.doc-see-also}

[](`~is_split_graph`) 

:   Split, with any edges between the clique and the independent set.

## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}

Every clique node is joined to all other nodes, and an independent-set node has a
non-neighbor unless it is the only one. So the graph is complete split exactly when the
nodes with fewer than $n - 1$ neighbors form an independent set, which is checked in
$O(V + E)$ time. Results match NetworkX pull request #8126's `is_complete_split_graph`.

## Examples {.doc-examples}

A star is a complete split graph: its center, a clique of one, is joined to every leaf.
A triangle with a pendant node is split, but `D` misses `A` and `B`:

::: {#778d60c5 .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import edgecase as ec

star = ec.Graph.from_edges([("A", "B"), ("A", "C"), ("A", "D")])
kite = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("A", "B"), ("A", "C"), ("B", "C"), ("C", "D")]
)

star.is_complete_split_graph(), kite.is_complete_split_graph()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}
```
(True, False)
```
:::
:::


### Hubs and spokes

A small airline flies between its two hubs and from each hub to each of four regional
airports, with no flights between the regional ones. The hubs reach every airport, and
the regional airports reach only the hubs:

::: {#14d9e75c .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
hubs, regional = ["Hub 1", "Hub 2"], ["Ayr", "Bray", "Cobh", "Dorn"]
routes = ec.Graph.from_edges(
    [("Hub 1", "Hub 2")] + [(h, r) for h in hubs for r in regional]
)
routes.set_node_attrs("kind", {**dict.fromkeys(hubs, "hub"),
                               **dict.fromkeys(regional, "regional")})
routes.is_complete_split_graph()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}
```
True
```
:::
:::


Dropping the route from Hub 1 to Dorn (right) leaves a split graph, with the hubs still a
clique and the regional airports still unlinked, but no longer a complete one:

::: {layout-ncol="2"}

::: {#2181ac27 .cell execution_count=3}
``` {.python .cell-code}
routes.layout_kamada_kawai().plot(
    layout="pos", color_by="kind", label_by="label",
    width=320, height=360, legend=False,
)
```

